  "result": "Looking at the code, the logic itself is sound: falsy check, strip, then truncate-or-return. One edge case worth flagging regardless of what the original looked like:\n\n**Whitespace-only names**: `not name` is checked *before* `.strip()`. So a name like `\"   \"` passes the falsy check (it's truthy), then gets stripped to `\"\"`, and since `len(\"\") > 20` is false, the function returns `\"\"` instead of `\"unknown\"`. If the original nested version stripped first and then checked emptiness, this would be a behavior change, not just a structural refactor.\n\nEverything else \u2014 the 20-char truncation with `\"...\"` appended, and returning the trimmed name otherwise \u2014 looks like a faithful early-return version of a typical if/elif/else.\n\nSince you didn't paste the \"before\" version, I can't do a side-by-side diff to confirm 100% equivalence \u2014 if you paste the original, I'll check line-by-line. Otherwise, the one thing I'd test explicitly before committing is `format_username(\"   \")`.",
